WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.900
Llandough hill · more than two thousand
years ago

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:09.133
[in Welsh] Will the weather hold today?
Smoke's going straight up. No wind,

3
00:00:09.173 --> 00:00:12.449
[in Welsh] no rain. We'll get the corn in.

4
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:16.028
[in Welsh] Heads only, mind. Cut them
high.

5
00:00:16.068 --> 00:00:19.560
[in Welsh] Leave the straw standing for
the cattle.

6
00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:32.657
[in Welsh] The boy's bringing the cattle
up off the marsh,

7
00:00:32.697 --> 00:00:35.060
[in Welsh] before the big tides come.

8
00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:39.489
[in Welsh] Good. Last autumn the water
came over the marsh in the night,

9
00:00:39.529 --> 00:00:41.820
[in Welsh] and we lost a calf.

10
00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:45.953
[in Welsh] Not this year. The pig's fat,
the store's full,

11
00:00:45.993 --> 00:00:50.313
[in Welsh] and the cloth's nearly done for
the little one's cloak.

12
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:04.063
[in Welsh] Listen, little one. Geese,
going over. The cold's coming.

13
00:01:04.103 --> 00:01:07.560
[in Welsh] Bring wood in by the door
tonight.

14
00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.483
[in Welsh] And before dark, a handful of
the new corn goes down to the spring.

15
00:01:12.523 --> 00:01:15.435
[in Welsh] It has always given us water.

16
00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:19.841
1979: a trench south of St Dochdwy's
church, Llandough,

17
00:01:19.881 --> 00:01:23.500
found a possible late Iron Age roundhouse.
(CH-0174)

18
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:27.006
They spoke Brittonic, the language Welsh
grew from.

19
00:01:27.046 --> 00:01:29.500
Here we hear it in modern Welsh.

